#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""premerge-check — required, scripted gate on a finished branch before it
merges into its target. Replaces "the orchestrating session remembers to
diff the branch against its target" with a deterministic check
(docs/reviews/retro-2026-07-12.md action item #3, proposal §4).

What: Runs the stale-base, contaminated-diff, and semantic gate checks on
      a finished branch and writes the premerge-check-ok marker on success.
When: Before every branch merge into its target.
By:   run by the merging session; enforced by block-dangerous-git, which
      denies `git merge` without the marker.

Catches the two contaminated diffs the 2026-07-12 session hit by hand
(`git diff --stat` run manually, after the fact) and turns that habit into
a required step run before every merge, not after a full agent run is
already finished. Also enforces semantic quality gates (CRAP score, mutation
testing, dependency rules) before merge (proposal: Agentic Quality Gates
§ Design 5 — Gate Execution Lifecycle).

Checks
  1. Stale-base signature: `<branch>` must contain every commit reachable
     from `<target>` (`git merge-base --is-ancestor <target> <branch>`). If
     not, the diff `<target>...<branch>` necessarily reverts lines
     `<target>` already has — the same underlying condition verify-base
     checks pre-dispatch, checked here again post-dispatch as a second,
     independent gate.
  2. File-count blowout: `git diff --name-only <target>...<branch>` must
     not exceed `--max-files` (default 20) — a large, unscoped diff is the
     signature of a stale-base run that touched far more than intended.
     On a story branch `story/<id>`, where `<id>` is a story ID or a
     finding ID, the unit's own files in `docs/reviews/` and
     `docs/findings/` do not count. A file there belongs to the unit when
     its name holds the ID or its `story` field names the ID. For a file
     that exists at the merge base, the field counts at the merge base, so
     a branch cannot take a file of another story by rewriting the field
     (IMPL-0027). For a file new on the branch, it counts at the head.
     A finding file whose `run` at the head names `<target>` also belongs to
     the story when a `file-qa-finding` entry of the story's stored
     `mutation-testing` result names it (BR-066).
  3. Out-of-scope paths (only if `--scope` given): every changed file must
     fall under at least one declared `--scope` prefix. The finding files of
     the story's stored `mutation-testing` result, as check 2 names them,
     are inside the scope.
  4. Code review ran (story branches only): a report
     `docs/reviews/code-review-*.md` at the branch head sets `story` to the
     story ID and `branch_head` to a full SHA that is an ancestor of the
     branch head (BR-122, IMPL-0025).
  5. Findings are closed (story branches only): no finding file
     `docs/findings/<ID>.md` at the branch head with `story` set to the
     story ID is open and blocking (BR-085). Register files never block.
     Checks 4 and 5 read the branch head through git, never the folder
     premerge-check runs in.
  6. Semantic gates (story branches only): if `<branch>` matches the pattern
     `story/<id>`, with a story ID or a finding ID, premerge-check adds a detached worktree at the branch
     head under `.current-work/<target>/premerge/<story-id>` of the primary
     checkout and removes it afterwards (BR-123, ADR-0038). Before the add,
     it removes a worktree that a crashed run left at that path. The gate
     list comes from the story file at the branch head (BR-066). Each gate
     is a hard gate — failure of any one blocks the merge.
       - `dependency-check`: when `docs/spec/dependency-rules.dsl` exists at
         the branch head; otherwise premerge-check prints the skip. Reruns
         in the worktree.
       - `test-design-verify`: when the story has a Test Design or Prior
         Tests heading. Reruns in the worktree.
       - Every entry under `gates` in `docs/testing.yaml` with
         `enabled: true`, plus each gate of the story's `quality-gates`
         field. The field only adds gates. premerge-check builds the calls
         of `crap-score` and `mutation-testing`; every other gate runs its
         `command` through the shell. A gate without an entry or without a
         command fails and is named. `crap-score` without an entry fails
         too, and the refusal names `setup-crap-score` (DEC-21). The old
         keys `crap_score`, `mutation_testing`, and `test_design_verify`
         count as the entries of their gates; with both forms, the key
         with hyphens wins with a warning (BR-055). No default picks a
         gate. When neither an entry nor the field names a built-in gate,
         that gate does not run, and one line names each such gate with
         the skill that sets it up: `setup-crap-score` for `crap-score`
         and `mutation-testing` for `mutation-testing`. The line is a
         notice and never blocks (DEC-33).
       - `crap-score` runs as `crap-score --base <target> --story-id <id>
         --report-dir <primary>/.current-work/crap-score/` in the worktree,
         with no list of files and no threshold. Its exit code decides, and
         the report of a refused merge stays in the primary checkout (BR-136).
       - `mutation-testing` passes after an exit 0. After a non-zero exit
         premerge-check takes the list `mutants` from the stored result
         `.current-work/mutation-testing/<story-id>.json` of the folder it
         runs in, and the rule `judge` of the `mutation-testing` script
         decides (BR-066, RL-G25).
       - The test suite: `test_command` of `docs/testing.yaml`. A non-zero
         exit fails and names the test suite. Without `test_command`,
         premerge-check prints the skip.
     Before the gates and the test suite, premerge-check runs the `ready`
     command of every measure of each gate that the merge runs, in the
     worktree. A gate with `enabled: false` that the story names counts
     (DEC-39). When one fails, neither the gates nor the test suite run, and
     the refusal names the measure and the command (BR-136).
     A gate cannot measure after a failed `ready` command or exit 2 of
     `crap-score`. premerge-check then exits 3 when every other check that
     ran passed, and its refusal starts with `GATE CANNOT MEASURE: <gate>`
     and holds the message. When another check failed too, it exits 1
     (BR-136, DEC-29).
     A built-in gate runs from `.agent-factory/factory/scripts/` of the
     primary checkout, or from the folder of premerge-check when that copy
     lacks the script. The worktree is its source root, and `uv` builds the
     worktree's own environment (OI-10). No file of a checkout changes, and
     no stash is made.
     `default_quality_gates` in `docs/charter/house-rules.md` is no gate
     source; when found, premerge-check prints a warning that names the file.
  7. Feature merge (`premerge-check dev <feature-branch>`; the target may
     also be `refs/heads/dev`, but not a SHA): the ledger
     `.current-work/<feature-branch>/dispatch-ledger.yaml` of the primary
     checkout holds no `failed` or `blocked` unit of work, no `pending` one
     that a return does not hold back (BR-113), and a wave record for each
     wave. At the branch head, the code-review report of the whole
     feature and the Fagan, security, and bug-hunt reports set `run` to the
     feature branch and `branch_head` to a full SHA that is an ancestor of
     the head (BR-122). No blocking finding file is open whose `run` names
     the feature branch or whose `story` names a unit of work of the run.
     `test_command` passes in a temporary worktree at
     `.current-work/<feature-branch>/premerge/feature`, which premerge-check
     removes afterwards (BR-123). The file limit applies only with
     `--max-files`, and no semantic gate runs.

Usage
  premerge-check <target> <branch> [--scope PATH ...] [--max-files N]

Exit codes
  0: PASS — no blocking finding
  1: BLOCK — at least one blocking finding; do not merge without an
     explicit override
  2: usage or git-invocation error
  3: GATE CANNOT MEASURE — a gate could not measure and every other check
     passed; `dispatch merge-story` sets the story `blocked` (BR-124)

On PASS, writes `.current-work/premerge-check-ok` at the invoking repo's
top level, keyed to `<branch>`'s current head SHA.
`.agent-factory/factory/config/hooks/block-dangerous-git.sh` denies `git merge <branch>`
unless that marker matches — mechanical enforcement, not just a prompt
instruction.
"""

from __future__ import annotations

import argparse
import json
import os
import re
import signal
import subprocess
import sys
import tempfile
import time
from importlib.machinery import SourceFileLoader
from importlib.util import module_from_spec, spec_from_loader
from pathlib import Path

SCRIPT_DIR = Path(__file__).resolve().parent
sys.path.insert(0, str(SCRIPT_DIR))

from dispatch_lib import Ledger, StoryState, _load_yaml, glob_match, held_back, stop_notice

try:
    import yaml
except ImportError:
    yaml = None  # type: ignore[assignment]

YAML_ERRORS: tuple[type[Exception], ...] = (ValueError, KeyError) + (
    (yaml.YAMLError,) if yaml else ()
)


def run_git(args: list[str]) -> tuple[int, str, str]:
    result = subprocess.run(["git", *args], capture_output=True, text=True, check=False)
    return result.returncode, result.stdout.strip(), result.stderr.strip()


def write_marker(branch: str) -> None:
    code, top, _ = run_git(["rev-parse", "--show-toplevel"])

    if code != 0:
        return

    _, head, _ = run_git(["rev-parse", branch])
    marker_dir = Path(top) / ".current-work"
    marker_dir.mkdir(exist_ok=True)
    (marker_dir / "premerge-check-ok").write_text(f"branch={branch}\nhead={head}\n")


def check_stale_base(target: str, branch: str) -> tuple[bool, str]:
    code, _, err = run_git(["merge-base", "--is-ancestor", target, branch])

    if code == 128:
        return False, f"ERROR: {err or 'unknown ref while checking ancestry'}"

    if code != 0:
        _, out, _ = run_git(
            ["rev-list", "--left-right", "--count", f"{target}...{branch}"]
        )
        left, _, _ = out.partition("\t")
        return False, (
            f"BLOCK stale-base-signature: {branch!r} is missing "
            f"{left.strip() or '?'} commit(s) present on {target!r} — the diff "
            f"necessarily reverts already-merged work"
        )

    return True, f"PASS stale-base-signature: {branch!r} contains all of {target!r}"


def changed_files(target: str, branch: str) -> tuple[int, list[str], str]:
    # --no-renames keeps the old name of a renamed file in the list, so a
    # renamed finding of another story still needs a scope entry (IMPL-0057).
    code, out, err = run_git(["diff", "--name-only", "--no-renames", f"{target}...{branch}"])

    if code != 0:
        return 2, [], err

    files = [line for line in out.splitlines() if line]
    return 0, files, ""


def check_blowout(
    files: list[str], max_files: int, uncounted: list[str] | None = None
) -> tuple[bool, str]:
    """Count the changed files without *uncounted*, the story's own files."""
    skipped = set(uncounted or [])
    count = len([f for f in files if f not in skipped])
    note = (
        f", not counting {len(skipped)} review or finding file(s) of the story"
        if skipped
        else ""
    )
    if count > max_files:
        return False, (
            f"BLOCK file-count-blowout: {count} files changed{note}, "
            f"exceeds --max-files {max_files}"
        )

    return (
        True,
        f"PASS file-count-blowout: {count} files changed{note} (limit {max_files})",
    )


def check_out_of_scope(
    files: list[str],
    scopes: list[str],
    scope_globs: list[str] | None = None,
) -> tuple[bool, str]:
    if not scopes and not scope_globs:
        return True, "SKIP out-of-scope-paths: no --scope declared"

    def _in_scope(f: str) -> bool:
        if any(f == s or f.startswith(s.rstrip("/") + "/") for s in scopes):
            return True
        return bool(scope_globs and any(glob_match(g, f) for g in scope_globs))

    out_of_scope = [f for f in files if not _in_scope(f)]

    if out_of_scope:
        listed = ", ".join(out_of_scope[:10])
        more = f" (+{len(out_of_scope) - 10} more)" if len(out_of_scope) > 10 else ""
        return False, f"BLOCK out-of-scope-paths: {listed}{more}"

    return (
        True,
        f"PASS out-of-scope-paths: all {len(files)} file(s) under declared scope",
    )


def extract_story_id(branch: str) -> str | None:
    """Extract the ID of a unit of work from its branch name: a story ID, as in
    'story/ST-0105' -> 'ST-0105', or a finding ID, as in 'story/BUG-0090'."""
    match = re.match(r"^story/([A-Z]+-\d+)$", branch)
    return match.group(1) if match else None


def _parse_yaml_frontmatter(frontmatter: str) -> dict:
    """Minimal YAML parser for story frontmatter. Handles lists and simple scalars."""
    result = {}
    lines = frontmatter.strip().split("\n")
    i = 0
    while i < len(lines):
        line = lines[i]
        if not line.strip() or line.strip().startswith("#"):
            i += 1
            continue

        # Parse key: value or key: [list items]
        if ":" in line:
            key, _, value = line.partition(":")
            key = key.strip()
            value = value.strip()

            # Handle list syntax: key: [item1, item2]
            if value.startswith("[") and value.endswith("]"):
                inner = value[1:-1].strip()
                result[key] = (
                    [item.strip() for item in inner.split(",") if item.strip()]
                    if inner
                    else []
                )
            # Handle list items on next lines
            elif value == "" or value.startswith("-"):
                items = []
                if value.startswith("-"):
                    items.append(value[1:].strip())
                i += 1
                while i < len(lines):
                    line = lines[i]
                    stripped = line.strip()
                    if stripped.startswith("-"):
                        items.append(stripped[1:].strip())
                        i += 1
                    elif stripped and not ":" in line:
                        i += 1
                    else:
                        break
                if items:
                    result[key] = items
                continue
            else:
                result[key] = value

        i += 1

    return result


def read_story_file(story_id: str, top: Path) -> dict | None:
    """Read story YAML frontmatter. Returns dict with parsed YAML or None."""
    story_file = top / "backlog" / f"{story_id}.md"
    if not story_file.exists():
        return None

    try:
        return parse_frontmatter(story_file.read_text())
    except OSError:
        return None


def parse_frontmatter(content: str) -> dict | None:
    """Return the YAML frontmatter between the `---` lines, or None without one."""
    if not content.startswith("---"):
        return None
    end_of_front = content.find("\n---\n", 3)
    if end_of_front == -1:
        return None
    frontmatter = content[3:end_of_front]
    try:
        # Try to use yaml library if available, fall back to simple parser
        data = yaml.safe_load(frontmatter) if yaml else _parse_yaml_frontmatter(frontmatter)
    except YAML_ERRORS:
        return None
    return data if isinstance(data, dict) else None


# --- Review and finding checks at the branch head (BR-085, BR-121, BR-122) ---

REVIEWS_DIR = "docs/reviews/"
FINDINGS_DIR = "docs/findings/"
REGISTER_DIR = "docs/findings/register/"
BLOCKING_CATEGORIES = ("defect", "risk")
BLOCKING_SEVERITIES = ("critical", "major")


class GitReadError(Exception):
    """git cannot read a file at the branch head."""


def files_at_head(branch: str, folder: str) -> list[str]:
    """The tracked files under *folder* at the head of *branch*."""
    # --full-tree reads *folder* from the top of the repository, also when
    # premerge-check runs in a subfolder (IMPL-0026).
    code, out, err = run_git(
        ["ls-tree", "-r", "--full-tree", "--name-only", branch, "--", folder]
    )
    if code != 0:
        raise GitReadError(f"git ls-tree {branch} {folder}: {err}")
    return [line for line in out.splitlines() if line]


def read_at_head(branch: str, paths: list[str]) -> dict[str, str]:
    """Read each of *paths* at the head of *branch* in one `git cat-file` call."""
    if not paths:
        return {}
    request = "".join(f"{branch}:{path}\n" for path in paths).encode()
    result = subprocess.run(
        ["git", "cat-file", "--batch"], input=request, capture_output=True, check=False
    )
    if result.returncode != 0:
        raise GitReadError(f"git cat-file --batch: {result.stderr.decode().strip()}")
    texts: dict[str, str] = {}
    out, pos = result.stdout, 0
    for path in paths:
        end = out.index(b"\n", pos)
        header = out[pos:end].decode()
        if header.endswith((" missing", " ambiguous")):
            raise GitReadError(f"git cat-file: {branch}:{path} {header.rsplit(' ', 1)[1]}")
        size = int(header.rsplit(" ", 1)[1])
        texts[path] = out[end + 1 : end + 1 + size].decode(errors="replace")
        pos = end + 1 + size + 1
    return texts


def parse_record(path: str, text: str) -> dict:
    """The fields of a review report, finding file, or register file."""
    if path.endswith(".json"):
        try:
            data = json.loads(text)
        except ValueError:
            return {}
        return data if isinstance(data, dict) else {}
    return parse_frontmatter(text) or {}


def _field(record: dict, key: str) -> str:
    value = record.get(key)
    return "" if value is None else str(value).strip()


def is_blocking(record: dict) -> bool:
    """The blocking rule of finding-classification.md (BR-085)."""
    return (
        _field(record, "category") in BLOCKING_CATEGORIES
        and _field(record, "severity") in BLOCKING_SEVERITIES
    )


def _is_finding_file(path: str) -> bool:
    """A finding file is `docs/findings/<ID>.md`, not a register file."""
    name = path[len(FINDINGS_DIR):] if path.startswith(FINDINGS_DIR) else ""
    return name.endswith(".md") and "/" not in name


def _records_with(branch: str, key: str, value: str, paths: list[str]) -> dict[str, dict]:
    """The records among *paths* whose field *key* is *value* at the head."""
    records = {}
    for path, text in read_at_head(branch, paths).items():
        if value not in text:
            continue
        record = parse_record(path, text)
        if _field(record, key) == value:
            records[path] = record
    return records


def _records_of_story(branch: str, story_id: str, paths: list[str]) -> dict[str, dict]:
    """The records among *paths* whose `story` field names *story_id*."""
    return _records_with(branch, "story", story_id, paths)


def merge_base(target: str, branch: str) -> str:
    """The merge base of *target* and *branch*; GitReadError without one."""
    code, out, err = run_git(["merge-base", target, branch])
    if code != 0 or not out:
        raise GitReadError(f"git merge-base {target} {branch}: {err or 'no merge base'}")
    return out


def _review_and_finding_files(rev: str) -> set[str]:
    return set(files_at_head(rev, REVIEWS_DIR) + files_at_head(rev, FINDINGS_DIR))


def story_owned_files(
    target: str, branch: str, story_id: str, files: list[str]
) -> list[str]:
    """The files of *files* in docs/reviews/ and docs/findings/ that belong to
    the unit of work: the name holds the ID, or the `story` field names it.

    For a file that exists at the merge base of *target* and *branch*, the
    field counts at the merge base, so a branch cannot take a file of
    another story by rewriting the field (IMPL-0027). For a file new on the
    branch, the field counts at the head of *branch*.
    """
    candidates = [f for f in files if f.startswith((REVIEWS_DIR, FINDINGS_DIR))]
    if not candidates:
        return []
    base = merge_base(target, branch)
    at_base = _review_and_finding_files(base)
    at_head = _review_and_finding_files(branch)
    by_name = [f for f in candidates if story_id in Path(f).name]
    rest = [f for f in candidates if f not in by_name]
    by_field = _records_of_story(base, story_id, [f for f in rest if f in at_base])
    by_field |= _records_of_story(
        branch, story_id, [f for f in rest if f not in at_base and f in at_head]
    )
    filed = mutant_finding_files(target, branch, story_id, [f for f in rest if f in at_head])
    return [f for f in candidates if f in by_name or f in by_field or f in filed]


# --- The stored result of mutation-testing (BR-066, RL-G25) ---

STORED_MUTATION_RESULTS = Path(".current-work") / "mutation-testing"


def _mutation_gate():
    """Load the mutation-testing script, which holds the result rule `judge`."""
    loader = SourceFileLoader("mutation_testing", str(SCRIPT_DIR / "mutation-testing"))
    module = module_from_spec(spec_from_loader("mutation_testing", loader))
    loader.exec_module(module)
    return module


def stored_mutation_result(story_id: str) -> dict:
    """The stored result of the gate-check step, or an empty mapping.

    It lies in `.current-work/mutation-testing/<story-id>.json` at the top of
    the folder premerge-check runs in, the invocation worktree in an
    orchestrated run and the primary checkout by hand.
    """
    code, top, _ = run_git(["rev-parse", "--show-toplevel"])
    if code != 0:
        return {}
    try:
        data = json.loads((Path(top) / STORED_MUTATION_RESULTS / f"{story_id}.json").read_text())
    except (OSError, ValueError):
        return {}
    return data if isinstance(data, dict) else {}


def filed_finding_ids(result: dict) -> set[str]:
    """The `finding` of each `file-qa-finding` entry of `mutants`."""
    mutants = result.get("mutants")
    return {
        str(m["finding"])
        for m in (mutants if isinstance(mutants, list) else [])
        if isinstance(m, dict)
        and m.get("resolution_action") == "file-qa-finding"
        and isinstance(m.get("finding"), str)
    }


def mutant_finding_files(
    target: str, branch: str, story_id: str, paths: list[str]
) -> set[str]:
    """The finding files of *paths* that the stored result names and whose
    `run` at the head of *branch* is *target* (BR-066)."""
    named = filed_finding_ids(stored_mutation_result(story_id))
    files = [f for f in paths if _is_finding_file(f) and Path(f).stem in named]
    return set(_records_with(branch, "run", target, files)) if files else set()


def filed_scope(story_id: str | None, owned: list[str], args: argparse.Namespace) -> list[str]:
    """The story's own files that its stored mutation result names; they are
    inside a declared scope (BR-066). Without a declared scope, none."""
    if not story_id or not (args.scope or args.scope_glob):
        return []
    named = filed_finding_ids(stored_mutation_result(story_id))
    return [f for f in owned if _is_finding_file(f) and Path(f).stem in named]


def finding_exists_at(branch: str):
    """Tell whether `docs/findings/<ID>.md` is at the head of *branch*."""
    def exists(finding_id: str) -> bool:
        return f"{FINDINGS_DIR}{finding_id}.md" in files_at_head(branch, FINDINGS_DIR)
    return exists


def _is_ancestor(commit: str, branch: str) -> bool:
    if not commit:
        return False
    code, _, _ = run_git(["merge-base", "--is-ancestor", commit, branch])
    return code == 0


FULL_SHA = re.compile(r"[0-9a-f]{40}")


def reviewed_report(branch: str, records: dict[str, dict]) -> tuple[str | None, list[str]]:
    """The first report of *records* that reviewed an ancestor of the head, and
    one problem per report whose `branch_head` is no full SHA (IMPL-0025).

    git resolves a ref name such as `story/ST-0350` to the current tip, which
    is always its own ancestor, so only a full 40-character SHA counts.
    """
    problems = []
    for path, record in sorted(records.items()):
        head = _field(record, "branch_head")
        if not FULL_SHA.fullmatch(head):
            problems.append(
                f"{path} has branch_head {head!r}, which is not a full 40-character SHA"
            )
        elif _is_ancestor(head, branch):
            return path, problems
    return None, problems


def _with_problems(line: str, problems: list[str]) -> str:
    return line + ("; " + "; ".join(problems) if problems else "")


def check_code_review(branch: str, story_id: str) -> tuple[bool, str]:
    """Pass when a code-review report of the story reviewed an ancestor of the head."""
    reports = [
        f for f in files_at_head(branch, REVIEWS_DIR)
        if Path(f).name.startswith("code-review-") and f.endswith(".md")
    ]
    path, problems = reviewed_report(branch, _records_of_story(branch, story_id, reports))
    if path is not None:
        return True, f"PASS code-review: {path} reviewed an ancestor of the head of {branch}"
    return False, _with_problems(
        f"BLOCK code-review: no report {REVIEWS_DIR}code-review-{story_id}-YYYY-MM-DD.md "
        f"at the head of {branch} has story {story_id} and a branch_head that is "
        f"an ancestor of the head (BR-122)",
        problems,
    )


def check_open_findings(branch: str, story_id: str) -> tuple[bool, str]:
    """Refuse while a blocking finding file of the story is open at the head."""
    finding_files = [f for f in files_at_head(branch, FINDINGS_DIR) if _is_finding_file(f)]
    open_ids = [
        _field(record, "id") or Path(path).stem
        for path, record in sorted(_records_of_story(branch, story_id, finding_files).items())
        if _field(record, "status") == "open" and is_blocking(record)
    ]
    if open_ids:
        return False, (
            f"BLOCK open-findings: {', '.join(open_ids)} of {story_id} "
            f"{'is' if len(open_ids) == 1 else 'are'} open and blocking at the head of {branch}"
        )
    return True, f"PASS open-findings: no open blocking finding of {story_id} at the head of {branch}"


# --- The feature merge: premerge-check dev <feature-branch> (BR-122, BR-123) ---

FEATURE_TARGET = "dev"
FEATURE_UNIT = "feature"
STOPPED_STATES = (StoryState.FAILED, StoryState.BLOCKED)


def is_feature_merge(target: str, branch: str) -> bool:
    """A branch other than a story branch that merges into dev is a feature merge."""
    return extract_story_id(branch) is None and names_dev(target)


def names_dev(target: str) -> bool:
    """True when *target* names the branch dev, such as `dev` or `refs/heads/dev`
    (IMPL-0052). A SHA is no name of dev, also when dev points at it: a wave base
    SHA can equal the head of dev (IMPL-0054)."""
    if target == FEATURE_TARGET:
        return True
    code, name, _ = run_git(["rev-parse", "--symbolic-full-name", target])
    return code == 0 and name == f"refs/heads/{FEATURE_TARGET}"


def ledger_path(primary: Path, feature_branch: str) -> Path:
    return primary / ".current-work" / feature_branch / "dispatch-ledger.yaml"


def check_run_state(path: Path, branch: str) -> tuple[list[tuple[bool, str]], list[str]]:
    """Check the units of work and the waves of the ledger at *path*.

    Return the results and the IDs of the units of work of the run.
    """
    try:
        ledger = Ledger.load(path)
    except FileNotFoundError:
        return [(False, (
            f"BLOCK run-state: no ledger {path}, so the check cannot show that "
            f"no unit of work of {branch} is failed or blocked"
        ))], []
    except (OSError, ValueError, KeyError, TypeError) + YAML_ERRORS as exc:
        return [(False, f"BLOCK run-state: cannot read the ledger {path}: {exc}")], []
    results = []
    stopped = [entry for entry in ledger.stories.values() if entry.status in STOPPED_STATES]
    for entry in stopped:
        reason = f" ({entry.reason})" if entry.reason else ""
        results.append((False, f"BLOCK run-state: {entry.id} is {entry.status.value}{reason}"))
    # A pending entry that a return does not hold back has not run yet. In a
    # run by hand it has no wave, so the wave check cannot see it (IMPL-0050).
    unstarted = [
        entry for entry in ledger.stories.values()
        if entry.status == StoryState.PENDING and not held_back(ledger, entry)
    ]
    for entry in unstarted:
        results.append((False, f"BLOCK run-state: {entry.id} is pending and has not run"))
    if not stopped and not unstarted:
        results.append((True, (
            f"PASS run-state: no unit of work of {branch} is failed, blocked, "
            f"or pending without a return"
        )))
    recorded = {record.number for record in ledger.waves}
    open_waves = sorted(
        {entry.wave for entry in ledger.stories.values() if entry.wave is not None} - recorded
    )
    for wave in open_waves:
        results.append((False, f"BLOCK waves: wave {wave} of {branch} has no wave record"))
    if not open_waves:
        results.append((True, f"PASS waves: every wave of {branch} has a wave record"))
    return results, list(ledger.stories)


def feature_reports(branch: str) -> list[tuple[str, str, str]]:
    """The name, file prefix and name pattern of each report of the feature checks."""
    review = f"code-review-{branch.replace('/', '-')}-"
    return [
        ("the end-of-feature review", review, f"{review}YYYY-MM-DD.md"),
        ("the Fagan report", "fagan-review-", "fagan-review-YYYY-MM-DD.md"),
        ("the security report", "security-review-", "security-review-YYYY-MM-DD.md"),
        ("the bug-hunt report", "bug-hunt-", "bug-hunt-YYYY-MM-DD.md"),
    ]


def check_feature_reports(branch: str) -> list[tuple[bool, str]]:
    """Refuse while a report of the feature checks is missing or outdated (BR-122)."""
    reviews = files_at_head(branch, REVIEWS_DIR)
    results = []
    for name, prefix, pattern in feature_reports(branch):
        candidates = [
            f for f in reviews if Path(f).name.startswith(prefix) and f.endswith(".md")
        ]
        path, problems = reviewed_report(
            branch, _records_with(branch, "run", branch, candidates)
        )
        if path is not None:
            results.append((True, (
                f"PASS feature-reports: {path} is {name} of {branch} "
                f"at an ancestor of the head"
            )))
            continue
        results.append((False, _with_problems(
            f"BLOCK feature-reports: {name} is missing: no report {REVIEWS_DIR}{pattern} "
            f"at the head of {branch} has run {branch} and a branch_head that is "
            f"an ancestor of the head (BR-122)",
            problems,
        )))
    return results


def check_feature_findings(branch: str, unit_ids: list[str]) -> tuple[bool, str]:
    """Refuse while a blocking finding of the run is open at the head (BR-085).

    A finding belongs to the run when its `run` names the branch or its
    `story` names a unit of work of the run.
    """
    finding_files = [f for f in files_at_head(branch, FINDINGS_DIR) if _is_finding_file(f)]
    units = set(unit_ids)
    named = []
    for path, text in sorted(read_at_head(branch, finding_files).items()):
        record = parse_record(path, text)
        if _field(record, "status") != "open" or not is_blocking(record):
            continue
        finding_id = _field(record, "id") or Path(path).stem
        if _field(record, "run") == branch:
            named.append(f"{finding_id} (run {branch})")
        elif _field(record, "story") in units:
            named.append(f"{finding_id} (story {_field(record, 'story')})")
    if named:
        return False, (
            f"BLOCK open-findings: {', '.join(named)} "
            f"{'is' if len(named) == 1 else 'are'} open and blocking at the head of {branch}"
        )
    return True, f"PASS open-findings: no open blocking finding of the run on {branch}"


def check_feature_merge(branch: str, primary: Path) -> list[tuple[bool, str]]:
    """Run the checks of the feature merge; the test suite runs in a temporary
    worktree at `.current-work/<feature-branch>/premerge/feature` (BR-123)."""
    results, unit_ids = check_run_state(ledger_path(primary, branch), branch)
    results += check_feature_reports(branch)
    results.append(check_feature_findings(branch, unit_ids))
    worktree = premerge_path(primary, branch, FEATURE_UNIT)
    added, err = add_worktree(worktree, branch)
    if not added:
        remove_worktree(worktree)
        raise GitReadError(f"cannot add worktree {worktree}: {err}")
    try:
        results.append(check_test_suite(worktree, branch, required=True))
    finally:
        remove_worktree(worktree)
    return results


# Gates whose call premerge-check builds; every other gate needs a command.
BUILT_IN_GATES = ("crap-score", "mutation-testing")
# Gates that run by their own condition and have no entry to pick (BR-066).
CONDITIONAL_GATES = ("dependency-check", "test-design-verify")
# Old keys with underscores and the gate each one names (BR-055).
OLD_GATE_KEYS = {
    "crap_score": "crap-score",
    "mutation_testing": "mutation-testing",
    "test_design_verify": "test-design-verify",
}
# The skill that sets up each built-in gate, named when its entry is missing (DEC-33).
SETUP_SKILLS = {"crap-score": "setup-crap-score", "mutation-testing": "mutation-testing"}
DEFAULT_MAX_FILES = 20
HOUSE_RULES = "docs/charter/house-rules.md"
TEST_DESIGN_HEADING = re.compile(r"^#{1,6}\s+(Test Design|Prior Tests)\s*$", re.MULTILINE)
DEPENDENCY_RULES = "docs/spec/dependency-rules.dsl"
TESTING_YAML = "docs/testing.yaml"
# The integration branch whose docs/testing.yaml decides whether the CRAP gate runs (DEC-68, DEC-80).
TRUSTED_BRANCH = "dev"
INSTALLED_SCRIPTS = Path(".agent-factory") / "factory" / "scripts"
# Variables that point uv at the caller's environment instead of the worktree's.
FOREIGN_ENVIRONMENT = ("VIRTUAL_ENV", "UV_PROJECT_ENVIRONMENT")


class BrokenTestingYaml(Exception):
    """docs/testing.yaml is not valid YAML, is empty, or is no mapping (BUG-0137).

    The message completes the sentence "docs/testing.yaml ... <message>".
    """


# _load_yaml raises TypeError for a file that is no mapping (BUG-0137).
_YAML_PARSE_ERRORS = (ValueError,) if yaml is None else (ValueError, yaml.YAMLError)


def _parse_testing_yaml(text: str) -> dict:
    """Return the text of docs/testing.yaml as a mapping.

    Raise BrokenTestingYaml when the text is not valid YAML, is empty, is no
    mapping, or has a `gates` section that is no mapping. A reader never gets a
    traceback for a broken file, and a broken `gates` never reads as "no gates"
    (BUG-0137, DEC-83).
    """
    try:
        data = _load_yaml(text)
    except TypeError as exc:
        raise BrokenTestingYaml(f"is empty or no mapping ({exc})") from exc
    except _YAML_PARSE_ERRORS as exc:
        raise BrokenTestingYaml("is not valid YAML") from exc
    gates = data.get("gates")
    if gates is not None and not isinstance(gates, dict):
        raise BrokenTestingYaml("has a gates section that is no mapping")
    return data


def _crap_score_is_broken(data: dict) -> bool:
    """Return True when gates.crap-score, or its old underscore key, is no mapping (BUG-0137)."""
    gates = data.get("gates")
    if not isinstance(gates, dict):
        return False
    present = [key for key in ("crap-score", "crap_score") if key in gates]
    return bool(present) and not isinstance(gates[present[0]], dict)


def _read_testing_text(path: Path) -> str:
    """Return the text of a docs/testing.yaml file as UTF-8 (BUG-0137).

    Raise BrokenTestingYaml when the file is not UTF-8. An OSError from the read
    still propagates, so the caller decides what a missing file means.
    """
    try:
        return path.read_text(encoding="utf-8")
    except UnicodeDecodeError as exc:
        raise BrokenTestingYaml("is not valid UTF-8") from exc


def _read_testing_yaml(top: Path) -> dict:
    """Return docs/testing.yaml as a mapping, or an empty mapping without one."""
    path = top / TESTING_YAML
    if not path.is_file():
        return {}
    try:
        text = _read_testing_text(path)
    except OSError:
        return {}
    return _parse_testing_yaml(text)


def gate_entries(top: Path) -> tuple[dict[str, dict] | None, list[str]]:
    """Return the entries of the `gates` section by gate name, and the warnings.

    An old key with underscores counts as the entry of its gate. With both
    forms, the key with hyphens wins and a warning names the old key (BR-055).
    A scalar such as `attempts` is no gate. Without a `gates` section, the
    entries are None.
    """
    return _entries_of(_read_testing_yaml(top))


def _entries_of(data: dict) -> tuple[dict[str, dict] | None, list[str]]:
    """The entries of the `gates` section of a parsed docs/testing.yaml (see gate_entries)."""
    gates = data.get("gates")
    if not isinstance(gates, dict):
        return None, []
    entries: dict[str, dict] = {}
    warnings: list[str] = []
    for key, value in gates.items():
        if not isinstance(value, dict) or key in OLD_GATE_KEYS:
            continue
        entries[str(key)] = value
    for old, name in OLD_GATE_KEYS.items():
        value = gates.get(old)
        if not isinstance(value, dict):
            continue
        if name in entries:
            warnings.append(
                f"WARN semantic-gates: {TESTING_YAML} has gates.{old} and gates.{name}; "
                f"premerge-check uses gates.{name}, so delete gates.{old}"
            )
            continue
        entries[name] = value
    return entries, warnings


def _quality_gates(story_data: dict | None) -> list[str] | None:
    """Return the story's `quality-gates` field as a list, or None without one."""
    if not story_data or story_data.get("quality-gates") is None:
        return None
    field = story_data["quality-gates"]
    return [str(g) for g in (field if isinstance(field, list) else [field])]


def _picked_gates(story_data: dict | None, entries: dict[str, dict] | None) -> list[str]:
    """Every enabled entry, then the gates the story's field adds (BR-066)."""
    field = _quality_gates(story_data)
    picked = [
        name
        for name, entry in (entries or {}).items()
        if entry.get("enabled") is True and name not in CONDITIONAL_GATES
    ]
    for name in field or []:
        if name not in picked and name not in CONDITIONAL_GATES:
            picked.append(name)
    return picked


def _house_rules_warning(top: Path) -> list[str]:
    path = top / HOUSE_RULES
    try:
        if path.is_file() and "default_quality_gates:" in path.read_text():
            return [
                f"WARN semantic-gates: {HOUSE_RULES} still has default_quality_gates; "
                "premerge-check ignores it and takes the gates from docs/testing.yaml"
            ]
    except OSError:
        pass
    return []


def _hardened_git_text(top: Path, *args: str) -> str | None:
    """The output of one git call under the hardening of crap-score (SEC-0009), or None.

    --no-replace-objects and core.useReplaceRefs=false keep a replace ref from
    standing in for a commit or blob of the branch.
    """
    proc = subprocess.run(
        ["git", "-c", "core.useReplaceRefs=false", "--no-replace-objects", *args],
        cwd=top, capture_output=True, check=False,
    )
    return proc.stdout.decode("utf-8", errors="replace") if proc.returncode == 0 else None


def trusted_crap_entry(top: Path) -> tuple[bool, dict | None]:
    """Return whether the branch dev exists, and its entry gates.crap-score (DEC-80).

    The commit is the one `show-ref --verify` names for refs/heads/dev (DEC-78),
    so no tag, remote ref or revision expression stands in for the branch.
    A dev without docs/testing.yaml has no entry. Raise BrokenTestingYaml when
    the file of dev is broken or its gates.crap-score is no mapping (BUG-0137).
    """
    tip = _hardened_git_text(top, "show-ref", "--verify", "--hash", f"refs/heads/{TRUSTED_BRANCH}")
    if tip is None:
        return False, None
    text = _hardened_git_text(top, "show", f"{tip.strip()}:{TESTING_YAML}")
    if text is None:
        return True, None
    data = _parse_testing_yaml(text)
    if _crap_score_is_broken(data):
        raise BrokenTestingYaml("has a gates.crap-score that is no mapping")
    entries, _ = _entries_of(data)
    return True, (entries or {}).get("crap-score")


def story_testing_yaml_refusal(top: Path, story_id: str) -> str | None:
    """Return the refusal line when the story's docs/testing.yaml is broken, or None (BUG-0137).

    The story must repair its own file, so the refusal stops the merge with exit 1
    and names the file. The semantic gates then do not run, and nothing is read
    from the broken file.
    """
    path = top / TESTING_YAML
    if not path.is_file():
        return None

    def refusal(problem: str) -> str:
        return (
            f"BLOCK testing-yaml: {TESTING_YAML} of {story_id} {problem}; the story must repair "
            "its own file, and the semantic gates did not run"
        )

    try:
        data = _parse_testing_yaml(_read_testing_text(path))
    except OSError:
        return None
    except BrokenTestingYaml as exc:
        return refusal(str(exc))
    if _crap_score_is_broken(data):
        key = "crap-score" if "crap-score" in data["gates"] else "crap_score"
        return refusal(f"has a gates.{key} that is no mapping")
    return None


def crap_cannot_measure(top: Path) -> tuple[str, str] | None:
    """Return the CRAP gate and its message when it cannot measure, or None (DEC-82, DEC-83).

    Without the branch dev, no branch decides whether the gate runs. An entry at
    the story head then cannot be measured, so the merge stops with exit 3. Without
    an entry at the head, the gate runs nowhere, as before. A broken file of dev
    cannot measure too, whatever the head holds, because the story cannot repair dev.
    """
    try:
        dev_exists, _ = trusted_crap_entry(top)
    except BrokenTestingYaml as exc:
        return (
            "crap-score",
            f"{TESTING_YAML} of the branch {TRUSTED_BRANCH} {exc}, so no setting of "
            f"{TRUSTED_BRANCH} decides whether the CRAP gate runs; repair the file on "
            f"{TRUSTED_BRANCH}",
        )
    entries, _ = gate_entries(top)
    if dev_exists or "crap-score" not in (entries or {}):
        return None
    return (
        "crap-score",
        f"the branch {TRUSTED_BRANCH} does not exist, so no branch decides whether the "
        f"CRAP gate runs, and {TESTING_YAML} of the story has gates.crap-score",
    )


def resolve_gates(top: Path, story_id: str) -> tuple[list[str], list[str]]:
    """Return the gates the merge requires, and any warnings.

    Whether crap-score runs, and whether its entry exists, come from dev, not from
    the story head (DEC-80), so a story cannot switch the gate off or on. The
    story's `quality-gates` field still adds the gate (BR-066).
    """
    entries, warnings = gate_entries(top)
    try:
        _, dev_crap_entry = trusted_crap_entry(top)
    except BrokenTestingYaml:
        # The broken file of dev is named by crap_cannot_measure (BUG-0137).
        dev_crap_entry = None
    deciding = dict(entries or {})
    if dev_crap_entry is None:
        deciding.pop("crap-score", None)
    else:
        deciding["crap-score"] = dev_crap_entry
    gates = _picked_gates(read_story_file(story_id, top), deciding)
    story_path = top / "backlog" / f"{story_id}.md"
    try:
        story_text = story_path.read_text() if story_path.is_file() else ""
    except OSError:
        story_text = ""
    if TEST_DESIGN_HEADING.search(story_text):
        gates.append("test-design-verify")
    if (top / DEPENDENCY_RULES).is_file():
        gates.append("dependency-check")
    return gates, warnings + _house_rules_warning(top)


def gate_refusal(gate: str, entries: dict[str, dict] | None) -> str | None:
    """Name why docs/testing.yaml cannot run *gate*, or None when it can.

    A built-in or conditional gate needs no command. `crap-score` still needs
    an entry, and its refusal names the skill that sets it up (DEC-21). Every
    other gate needs an entry with a command (BR-066, RL-G09, OI-15).
    """
    entry = (entries or {}).get(gate)
    if gate == "crap-score" and entry is None:
        return (
            f"no entry gates.{gate} in {TESTING_YAML}; "
            f"set the gate up with the skill {SETUP_SKILLS[gate]}"
        )
    if gate in BUILT_IN_GATES or gate in CONDITIONAL_GATES:
        return None
    if entry is None:
        return f"no entry gates.{gate} in {TESTING_YAML}"
    command = entry.get("command")
    if not isinstance(command, str) or not command.strip():
        return f"gates.{gate} in {TESTING_YAML} has no command"
    return None


def not_set_up_notice(top: Path, gates: list[str]) -> list[str]:
    """Name each built-in gate that has no entry and does not run (BR-066, DEC-20).

    For each gate, the line names the skill that sets it up. It is a notice:
    it never blocks the merge.
    """
    entries, _ = gate_entries(top)
    missing = [
        gate for gate in BUILT_IN_GATES if gate not in (entries or {}) and gate not in gates
    ]
    if not missing:
        return []
    parts = [f"{gate} (set up by the skill {SETUP_SKILLS[gate]})" for gate in missing]
    return [f"SKIP not-set-up gates: {TESTING_YAML} has no entry for {' or '.join(parts)}"]


def dependency_skip_notice(top: Path) -> list[str]:
    """Name the skip of dependency-check when *top* has no rules file (BR-066)."""
    if (top / DEPENDENCY_RULES).is_file():
        return []
    return [f"SKIP dependency-check: {DEPENDENCY_RULES} does not exist"]


def _describe_row(row: dict) -> str:
    name = row.get("function") or row.get("rule_name") or row.get("file") or "?"
    if "crap_score" in row:
        return f"{name} {row['crap_score']}"
    return str(name)


def read_gate_result(path: Path, branch: str | None = None) -> tuple[bool, str]:
    """Read one gate result file.

    crap-score and dependency-check write a list of rows with `pass_fail`;
    test-design-verify writes an object with `passed`. A result of
    mutation-testing, an object with `timed_out` or `mutants`, goes to the
    rule `judge`, which looks up each named finding at the head of *branch*.
    """
    try:
        data = json.loads(path.read_text())
    except (OSError, ValueError):
        return False, "invalid result"
    if isinstance(data, dict) and ("timed_out" in data or "mutants" in data):
        return judge_mutation_result(data, branch)
    if isinstance(data, list):
        failing = [r for r in data if not isinstance(r, dict) or r.get("pass_fail") != "pass"]
        if not failing:
            return True, "passed"
        return False, "failed: " + ", ".join(
            _describe_row(r) if isinstance(r, dict) else "invalid row" for r in failing
        )
    if isinstance(data, dict):
        return (True, "passed") if data.get("passed") is True else (False, "failed")
    return False, "invalid result"


def judge_mutation_result(data: dict, branch: str | None) -> tuple[bool, str]:
    """Apply the result rule of mutation-testing; without *branch* no finding exists."""
    exists = finding_exists_at(branch) if branch else (lambda _id: False)
    try:
        return _mutation_gate().judge(data, exists)
    except GitReadError as exc:
        return False, f"failed: {exc}"


def mutation_rerun_result(
    report: Path, story_id: str, branch: str | None
) -> tuple[bool, str]:
    """Judge the rerun after a non-zero exit with the stored list `mutants`.

    The rerun gives the exit code and the time-out. The gate-check step wrote
    the list into the stored result, which the rerun cannot hold (BR-066).
    """
    try:
        data = json.loads(report.read_text())
    except (OSError, ValueError):
        return False, "result not found"
    if not isinstance(data, dict):
        return False, "invalid result"
    stored = stored_mutation_result(story_id)
    data.pop("mutants", None)
    if "mutants" in stored:
        data["mutants"] = stored["mutants"]
    return judge_mutation_result(data, branch)


def primary_checkout() -> Path | None:
    """Return the top folder of the primary checkout, also from a linked worktree."""
    code, common, _ = run_git(["rev-parse", "--path-format=absolute", "--git-common-dir"])
    if code != 0:
        return None
    common_dir = Path(common)
    if common_dir.name == ".git":
        return common_dir.parent
    code, top, _ = run_git(["rev-parse", "--show-toplevel"])
    return Path(top) if code == 0 else None


def premerge_path(primary: Path, feature_branch: str, unit_id: str) -> Path:
    """Path of the temporary worktree of one unit of work (BR-123)."""
    return primary / ".current-work" / feature_branch / "premerge" / unit_id


def remove_worktree(path: Path) -> None:
    """Remove the worktree at *path*, if any, and prune its record (BR-123)."""
    run_git(["worktree", "remove", "--force", str(path)])
    run_git(["worktree", "prune"])


def add_worktree(path: Path, branch: str) -> tuple[bool, str]:
    """Remove a leftover at *path*, then add a detached worktree at *branch*'s head."""
    remove_worktree(path)
    code, _, err = run_git(["worktree", "add", "--detach", str(path), branch])
    return code == 0, err


def gate_script(primary: Path, gate: str) -> Path:
    """The installed copy of the primary checkout, else the folder of this script."""
    installed = primary / INSTALLED_SCRIPTS / gate
    return installed if installed.is_file() else SCRIPT_DIR / gate


def crap_report_dir(primary: Path) -> Path:
    """The folder of the report of crap-score in the primary checkout (BR-136)."""
    return primary / ".current-work" / "crap-score"


def gate_command(
    script: Path,
    gate: str,
    worktree: Path,
    story_id: str,
    target: str,
    primary: Path,
) -> list[str]:
    """Build the call of a gate that premerge-check names (BR-066, RL-G17).

    crap-score judges the functions changed since the merge base of *target*
    and writes its report into the primary checkout, so the report of a
    refused merge outlives the worktree (BR-136).
    """
    report_dir = worktree / ".current-work" / gate
    if gate == "dependency-check":
        return [
            str(script),
            "--dsl-path", str(worktree / DEPENDENCY_RULES),
            "--source-root", str(worktree),
            "--story-id", story_id,
            "--report-dir", str(report_dir),
        ]
    if gate == "crap-score":
        return [
            str(script),
            "--base", target,
            "--story-id", story_id,
            "--report-dir", str(crap_report_dir(primary)),
        ]
    if gate == "mutation-testing":
        return [
            str(script),
            "--story-id", story_id,
            "--testing-yaml", str(worktree / TESTING_YAML),
            "--report-dir", str(report_dir),
        ]
    return [
        str(script),
        "--story", str(worktree / "backlog" / f"{story_id}.md"),
        "--story-id", story_id,
        "--scope-map", str(worktree / "docs" / "spec" / "scope-map.md"),
        "--spec-dir", str(worktree / "docs" / "spec" / "features"),
        "--repo-root", str(worktree),
        "--epics", str(worktree / "backlog" / "epics.md"),
        "--report-dir", str(report_dir),
    ]


def _worktree_environment() -> dict[str, str]:
    """The caller's environment without the variables that point uv elsewhere."""
    return {k: v for k, v in os.environ.items() if k not in FOREIGN_ENVIRONMENT}


def _failure_detail(result: subprocess.CompletedProcess, report: Path | None) -> str:
    if report is not None and report.is_file():
        ok, detail = read_gate_result(report)
        if not ok and detail != "failed":
            return detail
    lines = (result.stderr or result.stdout).strip().splitlines()
    return f"exit {result.returncode}" + (f": {lines[-1]}" if lines else "")


def rerun_gate(
    primary: Path,
    gate: str,
    worktree: Path,
    story_id: str,
    entries: dict[str, dict] | None,
    target: str,
    branch: str | None = None,
) -> tuple[bool, str, str | None]:
    """Run *gate* in *worktree* and judge it by its exit code.

    The third value is the full message of a gate that could not measure,
    exit 2 of crap-score, and None otherwise (BR-136).

    After a non-zero exit of mutation-testing, the result rule decides with
    the stored list `mutants` (BR-066). After exit 1 of crap-score, its new
    report names the failing functions in the refusal. Every other exit of
    crap-score writes no report, so the report in the primary checkout is
    an earlier run's, and the refusal shows the message instead (IMPL-0071).

    premerge-check builds the call of a gate it names. Every other gate runs
    the `command` of its entry through the shell.
    """
    refusal = gate_refusal(gate, entries)
    if refusal is not None:
        return False, refusal, None
    env = _worktree_environment()
    if gate in BUILT_IN_GATES or gate in CONDITIONAL_GATES:
        script = gate_script(primary, gate)
        call: list[str] | str = gate_command(
            script, gate, worktree, story_id, target, primary
        )
        shell = False
        folder = crap_report_dir(primary) if gate == "crap-score" else worktree / ".current-work" / gate
        report: Path | None = folder / f"{story_id}.json"
    else:
        call = str((entries or {})[gate]["command"])
        shell = True
        report = None
    try:
        result = subprocess.run(
            call, shell=shell, cwd=worktree, env=env,
            capture_output=True, text=True, check=False,
        )
    except OSError as exc:
        return False, f"cannot run {call}: {exc}", None
    if result.returncode == 0:
        return True, "passed", None
    if gate == "mutation-testing" and report is not None:
        ok, detail = mutation_rerun_result(report, story_id, branch)
        return ok, detail if ok else f"exit {result.returncode}: {detail}", None
    if gate == "crap-score" and result.returncode != 1:
        report = None
    message = None
    if gate == "crap-score" and result.returncode == 2:
        message = (result.stderr or result.stdout).strip() or "no message"
    return False, _failure_detail(result, report), message


def check_semantic_gates(
    gates: list[str],
    story_id: str,
    worktree: Path,
    primary: Path,
    target: str,
    branch: str | None = None,
) -> tuple[list[str], tuple[str, str] | None]:
    """Run each gate in *worktree*.

    Return one entry per gate that fails, and the name and message of the
    gate that could not measure, or None (BR-136).
    """
    entries, _ = gate_entries(worktree)
    failed_gates = []
    cannot_measure = None
    for gate in gates:
        ok, detail, unmeasured = rerun_gate(
            primary, gate, worktree, story_id, entries, target, branch
        )
        if not ok:
            failed_gates.append(f"{gate} ({detail})")
        if unmeasured is not None:
            cannot_measure = (gate, unmeasured)
    return failed_gates, cannot_measure


def _ready_timeout(entry: dict) -> float | None:
    """The time limit of the gate in seconds, or None without a number."""
    minutes = entry.get("timeout_minutes")
    if isinstance(minutes, bool) or not isinstance(minutes, (int, float)):
        return None
    return float(minutes) * 60


# Seconds between SIGTERM and SIGKILL of a timed-out ready command, as in crap-score.
READY_STOP_GRACE = 5


def ready_commands(gates: list[str], entries: dict[str, dict] | None) -> list[tuple[str, str, str]]:
    """Return (gate, measure, command) for each `ready` command of the gates the merge runs.

    Only `crap-score` has measures, so only its entry is read (BR-136).
    """
    found = []
    for gate in gates:
        if gate != "crap-score":
            continue
        entry = (entries or {}).get(gate) or {}
        measures = entry.get("measures")
        for measure in measures if isinstance(measures, list) else []:
            if isinstance(measure, dict) and isinstance(measure.get("ready"), str):
                found.append((gate, str(measure.get("name")), measure["ready"]))
    return found


def run_ready_commands(worktree: Path, gates: list[str]) -> tuple[str, str] | None:
    """Run the `ready` commands before the test suite and the gates (BR-136, DEC-39).

    Return the gate and the message of the first command that fails, or
    None. A failed command stops the run, so the test suite and the gates
    do not run.
    """
    entries, _ = gate_entries(worktree)
    for gate, measure, command in ready_commands(gates, entries):
        entry = (entries or {}).get(gate) or {}
        label = f"the ready command of measure {measure} of gates.{gate}"
        try:
            code, message = _run_in_own_group(command, worktree, _ready_timeout(entry))
        except subprocess.TimeoutExpired:
            return gate, f"{label} ran out of timeout_minutes: {command}"
        except OSError as exc:
            return gate, f"cannot run {label}: {command}: {exc}"
        if code != 0:
            return gate, f"{label} exited {code}: {command}: {message}"
    return None


def _run_in_own_group(command: str, worktree: Path, timeout: float | None) -> tuple[int, str]:
    """Run *command* through the shell in its own process group (IMPL-0082).

    Return the exit code and the message of the command. At *timeout* the
    whole group gets SIGTERM, then SIGKILL after READY_STOP_GRACE seconds,
    as crap-score stops a command, and TimeoutExpired is raised. Output goes
    to temporary files, so a process that keeps a pipe open cannot block
    the wait.
    """
    with tempfile.TemporaryFile() as out, tempfile.TemporaryFile() as err:
        proc = subprocess.Popen(
            command, shell=True, cwd=worktree, env=_worktree_environment(),
            stdout=out, stderr=err, start_new_session=True,
        )
        try:
            code = proc.wait(timeout=timeout)
        except BaseException:
            _stop_group(proc)
            raise
        err.seek(0)
        out.seek(0)
        message = (
            err.read().decode(errors="replace").strip()
            or out.read().decode(errors="replace").strip()
            or "no message"
        )
    return code, message


def _signal_group(proc: subprocess.Popen, sig: int) -> None:
    try:
        os.killpg(proc.pid, sig)
    except ProcessLookupError:
        pass


def _group_alive(proc: subprocess.Popen) -> bool:
    try:
        os.killpg(proc.pid, 0)
    except ProcessLookupError:
        return False
    return True


def _stop_group(proc: subprocess.Popen) -> None:
    """Stop the command and every process of its group, as crap-score does.

    SIGTERM first, then SIGKILL after the grace period. The grace period
    lasts until every process of the group has ended, not only the shell.
    SIGKILL goes to the group even when the shell has exited, because its
    children may not have.
    """
    _signal_group(proc, signal.SIGTERM)
    deadline = time.monotonic() + READY_STOP_GRACE
    try:
        proc.wait(timeout=READY_STOP_GRACE)
        while _group_alive(proc) and time.monotonic() < deadline:
            time.sleep(0.1)
    except subprocess.TimeoutExpired:
        pass
    finally:
        _signal_group(proc, signal.SIGKILL)
        proc.wait()


def check_story_gates(
    gates: list[str],
    story_id: str,
    worktree: Path,
    primary: Path,
    target: str,
    branch: str,
) -> tuple[list[tuple[bool, str]], tuple[str, str] | None]:
    """Run the `ready` commands, the gates, and the test suite of a story merge.

    Return one (passed, line) per check, and the gate and message of a gate
    that could not measure, or None. A gate that could not measure is no
    failed check: premerge-check exits 3 when every other check passed
    (BR-136, DEC-29). After a failed `ready` command no gate and no test
    suite runs.
    """
    unmeasured = run_ready_commands(worktree, gates)
    if unmeasured is not None:
        return [(True, (
            f"BLOCK ready-commands: {unmeasured[1]}; the test suite and the gates "
            f"did not run for {story_id}"
        ))], unmeasured
    failed_gates, unmeasured = check_semantic_gates(
        gates, story_id, worktree, primary, target, branch
    )
    if failed_gates:
        line = f"BLOCK semantic-gates: {', '.join(failed_gates)} for {story_id}"
    elif gates:
        line = f"PASS semantic-gates: {', '.join(gates)} passed for {story_id}"
    else:
        line = f"PASS semantic-gates: no gate applies to {story_id}"
    # rerun_gate returns a message only for a gate that failed, and only
    # crap-score returns one. So the gate that could not measure stands in
    # failed_gates exactly once, and every other entry is a failed check.
    others_passed = len(failed_gates) == (0 if unmeasured is None else 1)
    return [(others_passed, line), check_test_suite(worktree, story_id)], unmeasured


def read_test_command(top: Path) -> str | None:
    """Return `test_command` of docs/testing.yaml, or None without one."""
    command = _read_testing_yaml(top).get("test_command")
    return command.strip() if isinstance(command, str) and command.strip() else None


def check_test_suite(
    worktree: Path, story_id: str, required: bool = False
) -> tuple[bool, str]:
    """Run the test suite in *worktree* with its own uv environment (OI-10).

    Without `test_command`, a story merge skips the suite. The feature merge
    passes *required*, because it must show that the full suite passes. A broken
    docs/testing.yaml refuses with BLOCK testing-yaml and no traceback, on the
    feature merge as on the story merge (BUG-0138, DEC-83).
    """
    try:
        command = read_test_command(worktree)
    except BrokenTestingYaml as exc:
        return False, (
            f"BLOCK testing-yaml: {TESTING_YAML} of {story_id} {exc}; the branch must "
            "repair its own file, and the test suite did not run"
        )
    if command is None and required:
        return False, (
            f"BLOCK test-suite: {TESTING_YAML} has no test_command, so the check "
            f"cannot run the test suite for {story_id}"
        )
    if command is None:
        return True, f"SKIP test-suite: {TESTING_YAML} has no test_command"
    try:
        result = subprocess.run(
            command, shell=True, cwd=worktree, env=_worktree_environment(),
            capture_output=True, text=True, check=False,
        )
    except OSError as exc:
        return False, f"BLOCK test-suite: cannot run {command!r}: {exc}"
    if result.returncode != 0:
        lines = (result.stdout + result.stderr).strip().splitlines()
        tail = f": {lines[-1]}" if lines else ""
        return False, (
            f"BLOCK test-suite: the test suite {command!r} exited "
            f"{result.returncode} for {story_id}{tail}"
        )
    return True, f"PASS test-suite: the test suite {command!r} passed for {story_id}"


def main(argv: list[str] | None = None) -> int:
    ap = argparse.ArgumentParser(description=__doc__.splitlines()[0])
    ap.add_argument("target", help="merge target branch, e.g. dev")
    ap.add_argument("branch", help="finished branch being considered for merge")
    ap.add_argument(
        "--scope",
        action="append",
        default=[],
        metavar="PATH",
        help="declared path prefix the dispatch said it would touch; repeatable",
    )
    ap.add_argument(
        "--scope-glob",
        action="append",
        default=[],
        metavar="PATTERN",
        help="gitignore-style glob pattern for scope matching; repeatable",
    )
    ap.add_argument(
        "--max-files",
        type=int,
        default=None,
        metavar="N",
        help="file-count blowout threshold (default 20; the feature merge has "
        "no limit without it)",
    )
    args = ap.parse_args(argv)

    code, _, err = run_git(["rev-parse", "--is-inside-work-tree"])

    if code != 0:
        print(f"premerge-check: not a git repository: {err}", file=sys.stderr)
        return 2

    # The stop on a critical finding comes before every other check. During
    # a stop, no other check runs and no marker is written (BR-091).
    notice = stop_notice()
    if notice is not None:
        sys.stdout.write(notice)
        return 1

    findings: list[bool] = []
    # (gate, message) of a gate that could not measure (BR-136).
    unmeasured: tuple[str, str] | None = None

    ok, line = check_stale_base(args.target, args.branch)
    print(line)
    findings.append(ok)

    status, files, err = changed_files(args.target, args.branch)

    if status == 2:
        print(f"premerge-check: {err}", file=sys.stderr)
        return 2

    story_id = extract_story_id(args.branch)
    try:
        owned = (
            story_owned_files(args.target, args.branch, story_id, files)
            if story_id
            else []
        )
    except GitReadError as exc:
        print(f"premerge-check: {exc}", file=sys.stderr)
        return 2

    feature_merge = is_feature_merge(args.target, args.branch)
    if feature_merge and args.max_files is None:
        print("SKIP file-count-blowout: the feature merge has no file limit without --max-files")
    else:
        max_files = DEFAULT_MAX_FILES if args.max_files is None else args.max_files
        ok, line = check_blowout(files, max_files, owned)
        print(line)
        findings.append(ok)

    ok, line = check_out_of_scope(
        files, args.scope + filed_scope(story_id, owned, args), args.scope_glob
    )
    print(line)
    findings.append(ok)

    if feature_merge:
        code, top, err = run_git(["rev-parse", "--show-toplevel"])
        if code != 0:
            print(f"premerge-check: {err}", file=sys.stderr)
            return 2
        try:
            results = check_feature_merge(args.branch, primary_checkout() or Path(top))
        except GitReadError as exc:
            print(f"premerge-check: {exc}", file=sys.stderr)
            return 2
        for ok, line in results:
            print(line)
            findings.append(ok)

    if story_id:
        try:
            for check in (check_code_review, check_open_findings):
                ok, line = check(args.branch, story_id)
                print(line)
                findings.append(ok)
        except GitReadError as exc:
            print(f"premerge-check: {exc}", file=sys.stderr)
            return 2

    # Check semantic gates if branch is a story branch
    if story_id:
        code, top, err = run_git(["rev-parse", "--show-toplevel"])
        if code != 0:
            print(f"premerge-check: {err}", file=sys.stderr)
            return 2
        primary = primary_checkout() or Path(top)
        worktree = premerge_path(primary, args.target, story_id)
        added, err = add_worktree(worktree, args.branch)
        if not added:
            remove_worktree(worktree)
            print(f"premerge-check: cannot add worktree {worktree}: {err}", file=sys.stderr)
            return 2
        results: list[tuple[bool, str]] = []
        try:
            refusal = story_testing_yaml_refusal(worktree, story_id)
            if refusal is not None:
                results = [(False, refusal)]
            else:
                gates, warnings = resolve_gates(worktree, story_id)
                notices = not_set_up_notice(worktree, gates)
                notices += dependency_skip_notice(worktree)
                for warning in warnings + notices:
                    print(warning)
                results, unmeasured = check_story_gates(
                    gates, story_id, worktree, primary, args.target, args.branch
                )
                unmeasured = unmeasured or crap_cannot_measure(worktree)
        finally:
            remove_worktree(worktree)
        for ok, line in results:
            print(line)
            findings.append(ok)
    elif not feature_merge:
        # Non-story branches skip semantic gates
        print("SKIP semantic-gates: branch does not match the pattern story/<id>")

    if all(findings) and unmeasured is None:
        print(
            f"premerge-check: PASS — {args.branch!r} clean to merge into {args.target!r}"
        )
        write_marker(args.branch)
        return 0

    # Exit 3 only when every other check that ran passed (BR-136, DEC-29).
    if all(findings):
        gate, message = unmeasured
        print(f"GATE CANNOT MEASURE: {gate}")
        print(message)
        print(
            f"premerge-check: CANNOT MEASURE — do not merge {args.branch!r}; "
            "dispatch merge-story sets the story blocked until the gate can measure"
        )
        return 3

    print(
        f"premerge-check: BLOCK — do not merge {args.branch!r} without an explicit override"
    )
    return 1


if __name__ == "__main__":
    sys.exit(main())
